CONF68K -- an OS-9/68000 conformance suiteWhat this is: one hundred and sixteen standalone tests, each checking one documentedMicroware OS-9/68000 behaviour against what this system actually does.Every test prints exactly one RESULT line. DOCS/claims.md records themanual page behind each one. This is not a regression suite for anyparticular emulator -- it exists so that a real OS-9/68k system and thedocumentation can be compared, and any disagreement found.HOW TO RUN ITFrom an OS-9 shell, with this directory copied onto a disk the system canreach -- below it is assumed to be at /h1/CONF68K, so substitute your owndevice and path:    chd /h1/CONF68K    chx /h1/CONF68K/CMDS    runall >>+RESULTS/errorsThat is the whole procedure. There is nothing to load first, no runtime toinstall, and no configuration to edit. At the end tally says, in plainwords, whether there is anything to send us: usually there is not.WITHOUT A SHELL (os9exec on Windows, macOS or Linux, or in a browser)runall needs a shell, and the shell is Microware's. The run module doeswhat runall says without one, so the suite runs with nothing but thisdisk. With os9exec and the single-disk image (below):    OS9DISK=/full/path/to/conf68k.dsk  os9exec -r /dd/CMDS/run(on Windows: set OS9DISK=C:/where/it/is/conf68k.dsk, thenos9exec -r /dd/CMDS/run). It prints a dot per test and then tally'sverdict. From the image every test runs, so all but t49 pass there (t49measures a `load` this disk does not carry); run from a plain host folderinstead, the record-locking tests report SKIP, which isnormal and says so.The >>+RESULTS/errors keeps the shell's own messages. A test that diesoutright -- a bus error, an illegal instruction -- prints no RESULT line,and the only record of WHY is the error the shell prints for it. Withoutthe redirection that goes to your screen and is lost.The chx line is what makes the tests findable by bare name; the chd line iswhat makes them write their results into this directory. Both are needed.Note that chx changes YOUR shell's execution directory, so ordinarycommands from your own command directory will not be found by bare nameuntil you set it back:    chx /dd/CMDSor whatever your command directory is. Until then, reach a utility by itsfull path -- reading the report, for instance:    /dd/CMDS/list RESULTS/reportWHAT IT NEEDSA kernel and a shell. Nothing else.One exception, and it costs you nothing: t49 checks that `load` puts amodule in the module directory, and `load` is a UTILITY rather than partof this suite. If a `load` is present in CMDS the test runs it and gives averdict; if there is none it reports SKIP carrying 221 (E$MNF), the codeF$Fork gave when it could not find one. A `load` built against the `cio`trap handler needs cio in CMDS as well, and the harness that installs oneinstalls both -- half an installation would report a missing trap handleras though it were a defect in the module directory. Nothing else in the suite dependson it, and no other test changes behaviour because of it.Every module here is hand-written 68000 assembly that calls the kerneldirectly. Nothing links against the C library, no trap handler isinstalled, and no BASIC or RunB is involved -- so the suite runs on asystem with no development tools and no language runtime installed at all.Even the run-boundary line and the result counter are our own modulesrather than the system's echo and a packed BASIC09 program, specifically sothat pointing chx at this disk cannot make some utility unfindable partwaythrough a run.About 80K of free space on the device it runs from. One test (t07) checksthat a write past a seek beyond 65535 extends a file correctly, which meansactually creating a file of about 70K. If the space is not there that testreports ERROR, not FAIL -- see below.TESTS THAT NEED TWO PROCESSESt01 to t18, t34 to t40 and t49 to t65, t68 to t85, t87 to t103 each run as a single process -- t49 forks`load` and waits for it, but the test itself is one process and needs nohandshake. t66, t67 and t86 fork a child that only sleeps and exits, so theparent can signal it; no handshake is needed there either. t114 forks achild that only links a module and exits with what that gave; t115forks one that spins without a system call until it is signalled.t19 to t31, and t116 (t23 with the holder writing only), cannot: they are aboutrecord locking, and every one of them is a claim about what one processsees while another holds something. A process is exempt from its own locks-- "RBF notices that the same process owns both paths and keeps them fromlocking each other out" -- so a single-process version of these tests wouldmeasure nothing and pass on a system with no locking at all.Each of those tests is ONE module that runs twice. It forks itself, passinga one-byte parameter that tells the second instance to take the other halfof the test, so there are no helper modules to install or keep in step.t41 needs two processes for a different reason. Ev$Pulse restores theevent value as soon as its signal search is over, so the value it setsexists only for processes ALREADY waiting -- one process can never catcha pulse, however quickly it looks. It is built on the same harness, butthe order is inverted: the CHILD has to be blocked before the PARENTacts, where every locking test has the parent act first. Nothing in OS-9reports "I am asleep now", so it uses the same readiness marker and theparent's own timeout catches a pulse that arrived too early, reporting902 instead of hanging. t42 is the same shape with an ordinaryEv$Signl in place of the pulse -- the common case, where t14 checksonly the arithmetic and has nobody waiting. Neither needs RBF, andneither ever reports SKIP.Two things about the LOCKING tests are worth knowing before reading a report:  One test outside that group also answers by device: t95 (the SS_Size  SetStat) is SKIP on a host directory and PASS on an image, which is why  DOCS/expected and DOCS/expected-rbf are separate files. t99 used to be a  second such case -- a host directory would delete a file that was open --  until that was fixed on 2026-09-21; both devices refuse now.  They report SKIP unless the device really is RBF. A host-native directory  presented by an emulator has no allocation map and no locks, and a verdict  earned there would say nothing about OS-9.  The holder rescues the contender rather than relying on SS_Ticks. RBF's  documented default is to sleep forever on a locked record, and SS_Ticks is  the documented way to ask for an error instead -- but a suite that RELIED  on it could only run on a system that already implements it. So the holder  releases everything on a timer and, if the contender still has not  finished, kills it and reports 902. Two observed values are the suite's  own rather than the system's: 901 means the access succeeded only after  the holder let go, and 902 means it never returned at all. Neither is an  OS-9 error code.These tests write a few marker files into SCRATCH/ to synchronise. Likeeverything else they clean up after themselves, and like everything elsethey touch nothing outside this directory.It is deliberately plain 68000 code: no instruction beyond the original68000 is used, and no 68020-and-later addressing mode, so a prebuilt modulehere runs on any processor OS-9/68k itself runs on.WHAT A RESULT LINE MEANS    RESULT tNN VERDICT  obs=NNNNNN exp=NNNNNN  what was checkedPASS   the documented behaviour is what this system did.FAIL   a real disagreement: the system did something the cited manual       passage says it should not. This is the most useful thing this       suite can tell you.ERROR  the test could not set up the situation it meant to check -- its       own create, open or write failed -- so it has no verdict to give.       obs= carries the error code that stopped it. A full disk shows up       here. ERROR is not FAIL.SKIP   the test could not exercise its claim on this run at all. The       record-locking tests (t19 to t31) report this on a device with no       record locking underneath it, carrying the code the device gave in       obs=. A SKIP is not a weaker PASS and must not be read as one: it       means the claim was not checked here. Run those on a real disk.       t49 reports it for the other reason a claim can go unchecked: the       `load` it measures was not installed, so there was nothing to ask.obs= is what this system did, exp= is what the manual says. Both are sixdigits, zero-padded, so report lines line up column-for-column and tworuns can be compared with a plain file diff. Six because a 68k fileposition is a full 32-bit value and t07 works above 65535 on purpose; avalue too large for the field prints as OVRFLW rather than as its own lowsix digits, because a wrong number that looks plausible is worse than anobvious marker.Three other kinds of line can appear, and none is counted by tally:  INFO     the first lines of a run, from mark: what the run happened on.  DETAIL   printed just before a FAIL's RESULT line by a test whose           failing value will not fit in obs= -- the integer an FPU stored,           an address, the raw bytes of an event table entry. A PASS never           prints one. They are what lets a disagreement be understood from           the report alone.  RUN      the run boundary, as described below.The two-process tests (t19 to t31, t33, t41, t42, t104 to t106, t116) say in anERROR's obs= whose failure it was: under 900 is the parent's own errorcode; 900 means F$Wait kept waking for something that was not the child;2000 plus a code means the child could not set up, and 3000 plus a statusmeans the child DIED with that status (3102 is a bus error). 901, 902 and903 are observations, not errors: see DOCS/claims.md.The FPU tests (t107 to t109, t112) report SKIP when an FPU instructiontraps as unimplemented. obs= then holds the digits already scored beforethe trap, so a partial FPU -- a 68040 or 68060 without its supportpackage -- shows how far it got. 0 means the first instruction trapped.If one test cannot be found or dies outright, only that test is missing:runall's first line is "set nx", which tells the shell to carry on past afailed command instead of abandoning the procedure. Without it a singleunfindable module costs you every test after it as well, and the run looksfar more broken than it is. This was measured, not assumed.A test's verdict is the line it prints, never its exit status. A test thatdied without printing simply has no line in the report, and the totals willbe short -- which is the intended way to notice.WHAT IT WRITESEverything stays inside this directory: RESULTS/report gets the runboundary line and one RESULT line per test, and SCRATCH/ gets whateverworking files the tests create. Nothing outside is touched, and no testwrites to /dd.Each test deletes its own scratch file before creating it, so a rerun isthe same test as the first run rather than a different one.RESULTS/report is append-only. Each run writes a "RUN prebuilt" linebefore anything else, and that boundary is the only thing that lets alater reader split a report containing several runs back into separateruns. Rerunning is just runall again.tally reads RESULTS/report and prints:    CONF68K totals: PASS=nnnnnn FAIL=nnnnnn SKIP=nnnnnn ERROR=nnnnnnand then what to do about it. It counts the latest run only, from its RUNline, so an old run's results never colour a new one. runall ends byrunning it for you, and so does run.AS A SINGLE DISKThis directory can also be handed over as ONE RBF disk image, which isusually the easier thing to carry to a real machine:    tools/selfhost68k/build-image.sh      (from the os9exec repo)That writes build/selfhost68k/conf68k.dsk -- an OS-9 RBF filesystemholding CMDS/, DOCS/, runall and this readme, with SCRATCH/ and RESULTS/already made. Put it on a disk device your system can mount, then:    chd /<device>    chx /<device>/CMDS    runall >>+RESULTS/errorsexactly as above. Nothing else is needed: every module on it is plain68000 assembly that calls the kernel directly, so a system with no SDKand no language runtime installed can still run the whole suite.tools/selfhost68k/verify-image.sh checks a built image two ways -- everyfile copied back off it and compared byte for byte, and all 116 tests runfrom it -- because a disk that lists correctly and cannot run is thefailure worth catching.REBUILDING FROM SOURCECMDS/ ships prebuilt so the suite runs with nothing but a shell. SRC/carries the exact sources those modules were built from, includingreport.i (the result-line emitter), verdict.i (the shared verdict tailevery test includes) and fork.i (the two-process harness t19 to t27 arebuilt on).To rebuild you need this system's own r68 and l68. For each test:    chd /h1/CONF68K/SRC    r68 t01open.a -o=../SCRATCH/t01open.r    l68 ../SCRATCH/t01open.r -o=../REBUILT/t01openWrite rebuilt modules to REBUILT/, never into CMDS/. If your assemblerproduces something that will not run, you still have a working prebuiltCMDS/ as well as whatever REBUILT/ turned out to be -- which is the casemost worth hearing about, so please do report it.Give l68's -o= an ABSOLUTE path, as shown above. This is the one place thesuite cannot keep its promise of relative paths only, and the reason is worthknowing before it costs you something: l68 resolves a relative -o= againstyour EXECUTION directory, not the data directory it read the .r file from. Abare "-o=t01open" therefore writes the module into your system commanddirectory. That is also why no rebuild procedure ships here -- a procedurefile cannot name its own output location without already knowing the absolutepath it was installed at, and guessing wrong means writing into /dd/CMDS.Measured, not inferred.Once REBUILT/ is populated, run the rebuilt suite by pointing chx at it:    chd /h1/CONF68K    chx /h1/CONF68K/REBUILT    runall >>+RESULTS/errorsr68 prints "destination in short branch range" a number of times during arebuild. That is advisory, not a problem: it means a branch could have useda shorter form. These sources use the word form wherever the distancedepends on how long a particular test's body is, because a form that iscorrect in every test is worth more than one that is optimal in someof them and out of range in others.Line endings here are CR, as OS-9 requires. If you copy this directorythrough a host system, make sure that whatever you copy it with has not"helpfully" converted the sources or the runall procedure to LF endings: aprocedure file with LF endings makes the shell echo the whole file and runnothing, reporting no error at all, and an assembler source with LF endingsreads as one enormous line.SENDING RESULTS BACK -- ONLY IF TALLY ASKSIf tally says "Nothing to send", there is nothing to send: we only want tohear when something does not pass. SKIP does not count; a SKIP means theclaim cannot be checked on your system or disk, and is not a failure.If tally asks, open an issue at    https://github.com/peacedudes/os9exec/issuesand paste in the whole of RESULTS/report, and RESULTS/errors if the runmade one, rather than a hand-trimmed excerpt. Its first lines, the onesstarting INFO, already say what it ran on: the OS-9 version and edition,the processor and FPU, the tick rate, the user, the device and its type,and the date. A sentence on the machine itself helps -- which board orcomputer, real hardware or an emulator -- since no call reports that.On real hardware, two things let one run check everything: run it as thesuper user (group 0; t113 reports SKIP for anybody else), and from a realRBF disk -- a hard disk, floppy or RAM disk, not a network or host-shareddirectory, where the record-locking tests (t19 to t31, t104 to t106, t116) andt95 report SKIP.DOCS/ourruns.md records what this suite does on the systems we can reach,which is what your run can be compared against.